[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002581","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_002581","source_record_id":"rec_002581","source_corpus":"include","title":"CellReasoner: A reasoning-enhanced large language model for cell type annotation","abstract":"We present CellReasoner, a lightweight, open-source large language model (LLM) tailored for single-cell type annotation. We introduced a compact training strategy that activates the reasoning capabilities of 7B-parameter LLMs using only 380 high-quality chain-of-thought exemplars. CellReasoner directly maps cell-level gene expression profiles to cell type labels, exhibiting robust zero- and few-shot generalization. The model further demonstrates expertlevel, marker-by-marker reasoning, enabling structured, interpretable annotations and offering a practical solution for intelligent single-cell analysis.","doi":"10.1101/2025.05.20.655112","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"full_text_context":"[abstract: Abstract]\nWe present CellReasoner, a lightweight, open-source large language model (LLM) tailored for single-cell type annotation. We introduced a compact training strategy that activates the reasoning capabilities of 7B-parameter LLMs using only 380 high-quality chain-of-thought exemplars. CellReasoner directly maps cell-level gene expression profiles to cell type labels, exhibiting robust zero- and few-shot generalization. The model further demonstrates expertlevel, marker-by-marker reasoning, enabling structured, interpretable annotations and offering a practical solution for intelligent single-cell analysis.\n\n[data_representation: Dataset Construction]\n- To support single-cell annotation, we designed two distinct task formulations, each guided by 248\n- a specific instruction to steer the model's reasoning behavior. 249\n- Task 1 - Direct Annotation Task 250\n- The first task instructs the model to infer the cell type directly based on marker gene expression: 251\n- Instruction 1: \"These are highly expressed genes within a certain type of tumor cells; please 252\n- annotate the cell type based on these marker genes. Please infer the cell type based on the 253\n- marker genes, and directly give the final annotation result.\" 254\n- In this task, the model is expected to generate a cell type label directly from the input cell 255\n- sentence containing highly expressed genes. The task is formally defined as: 256\n- 𝑇𝑇𝑎𝑎𝑠𝑠𝑘𝑘1 = { 257\n\n```\ncell: 𝑐𝑐 𝑖𝑖 , 258 259 input: 𝐶𝐶 𝑖𝑖 , 260 output: a 261 } 262\n```\n\n284 different annotation paradigms-ranging from direct classification to 285 explainable predictions. 286\n\n```\ninstruction:Instruction1, where 𝐶𝐶 𝑖𝑖 denotes the cell sentence for cell 𝑐𝑐 𝑖𝑖 , and 𝑎𝑎 ∈ 𝒜𝒜 is the predicted cell type label. This 263 task assesses the model's ability to map cell sentence directly to a structured annotation output. 264 265 Task 2 - Reasoning-Augmented Annotation Task 266 The second task extends the first by requiring an explicit reasoning chain enclosed within 267 specific tags: 268 Instruction 2: \"These are highly expressed genes within a certain type of tumor cells; please 269 annotate the cell type based on these marker genes. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Training Dataset]\nWe curated expert-annotated single-cell transcriptomic data from multiple high-quality pancancer datasets, encompassing a wide range of tumor and immune cell types. In total, 38 distinct cell types were included ( Supplementary Table 3 ). To support model training and evaluation, we constructed multiple datasets following the strategy below:\n\nFirst, to support reasoning ability learning, we constructed a high-quality CoT dataset by randomly sampling 10 cells from each of the 38 cell types, resulting in 380 carefully selected samples ( Supplementary Table 4 ). During the sampling process, we enforced a constraint that the gene-word overlap between any two cell sentences must not exceed 50%, in order to enhance sample diversity and improve reasoning generalizability.\n\n387\n\n388\n\n389\n\n390\n\n391\n\n392\n\n393\n\n394\n\n395\n\n396\n\n397\n\n398\n\n399\n\n400\n\n401\n\n402\n\n403\n\n404\n\n405\n\n406\n\n407\n\n408\n\n409\n\n410\n\n411\n\n412\n\n413\n\n414\n\n415\n\n416\n\n417\n\n418\n\n419\n\n420\n\nSecond, to support training in the knowledge infusion stage, we randomly selected 1,000 cells per cell type, resulting in a pancancer38k training dataset consisting of 37,187 cells ( Supplementary Table 5 ). For cell types with fewer than 1,000 available cells, we retained 100 cells for internal evaluation and used the remainder for training.\n\nFinally, we randomly selected an additional 3,800 cells to construct an internal test set ( Supplementary Table 6 ), ensuring that there was no overlap with the training data. This dataset was used for downstream model evaluation and analysis. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: Datasets]\n### PDAC dataset\n\nThe PDAC dataset (Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma) comprises 57,702 single-cell transcriptomes encompassing 51 distinct cell subtypes. It includes a broad spectrum of immune and non-immune cell types, such as CD8+ T cells, NKT cells, CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, monocytes, neutrophils, fibroblasts, epithelial cells (EC), endothelial cells, and B cells. The dataset also contains various macrophage and T cell subsets, endocrine cells, acinar cells, T follicular helper (TFH) cells, and mast cells. Notably, macrophage subtypes include LAM1-4, M1-like, and M2-like macrophages.\n\n### PBMC3K dataset\n\nThe PBMC3K dataset contains single-cell RNA-seq data from 2,700 peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) derived from a healthy human donor. The dataset includes major immune cell populations such as naïve CD4+ T cells, memory CD4+ T cells, CD14+ monocytes, B cells, CD8+ T cells, FCGR3A+ monocytes, natural killer (NK) cells, dendritic cells (DCs), and platelets.\n\n### Multiome PBMC dataset\n\nThis dataset contains paired single-cell RNA and ATAC-seq profiles from human PBMCs, generated using the 10x Genomics Single Cell Multiome platform. [truncated]\n\n[data_representation: dataset]\nThe PDAC dataset (Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma) comprises 57,702 single-cell transcriptomes encompassing 51 distinct cell subtypes. It includes a broad spectrum of immune and non-immune cell types, such as CD8+ T cells, NKT cells, CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, monocytes, neutrophils, fibroblasts, epithelial cells (EC), endothelial cells, and B cells. The dataset also contains various macrophage and T cell subsets, endocrine cells, acinar cells, T follicular helper (TFH) cells, and mast cells. Notably, macrophage subtypes include LAM1-4, M1-like, and M2-like macrophages.","section_evidence":[{"section_type":"abstract","heading":"Abstract","heading_level":2,"line_start":99,"line_end":102,"text":"We present CellReasoner, a lightweight, open-source large language model (LLM) tailored for single-cell type annotation. We introduced a compact training strategy that activates the reasoning capabilities of 7B-parameter LLMs using only 380 high-quality chain-of-thought exemplars. CellReasoner directly maps cell-level gene expression profiles to cell type labels, exhibiting robust zero- and few-shot generalization. The model further demonstrates expertlevel, marker-by-marker reasoning, enabling structured, interpretable annotations and offering a practical solution for intelligent single-cell analysis.","original_chars":609},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Dataset Construction","heading_level":2,"line_start":542,"line_end":634,"text":"- To support single-cell annotation, we designed two distinct task formulations, each guided by 248\n- a specific instruction to steer the model's reasoning behavior. 249\n- Task 1 - Direct Annotation Task 250\n- The first task instructs the model to infer the cell type directly based on marker gene expression: 251\n- Instruction 1: \"These are highly expressed genes within a certain type of tumor cells; please 252\n- annotate the cell type based on these marker genes. Please infer the cell type based on the 253\n- marker genes, and directly give the final annotation result.\" 254\n- In this task, the model is expected to generate a cell type label directly from the input cell 255\n- sentence containing highly expressed genes. The task is formally defined as: 256\n- 𝑇𝑇𝑎𝑎𝑠𝑠𝑘𝑘1 = { 257\n\n```\ncell: 𝑐𝑐 𝑖𝑖 , 258 259 input: 𝐶𝐶 𝑖𝑖 , 260 output: a 261 } 262\n```\n\n284 different annotation paradigms-ranging from direct classification to 285 explainable predictions. 286\n\n```\ninstruction:Instruction1, where 𝐶𝐶 𝑖𝑖 denotes the cell sentence for cell 𝑐𝑐 𝑖𝑖 , and 𝑎𝑎 ∈ 𝒜𝒜 is the predicted cell type label. This 263 task assesses the model's ability to map cell sentence directly to a structured annotation output. 264 265 Task 2 - Reasoning-Augmented Annotation Task 266 The second task extends the first by requiring an explicit reasoning chain enclosed within 267 specific tags: 268 Instruction 2: \"These are highly expressed genes within a certain type of tumor cells; please 269 annotate the cell type based on these marker genes. [truncated]","original_chars":2529},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Training Dataset","heading_level":2,"line_start":815,"line_end":892,"text":"We curated expert-annotated single-cell transcriptomic data from multiple high-quality pancancer datasets, encompassing a wide range of tumor and immune cell types. In total, 38 distinct cell types were included ( Supplementary Table 3 ). To support model training and evaluation, we constructed multiple datasets following the strategy below:\n\nFirst, to support reasoning ability learning, we constructed a high-quality CoT dataset by randomly sampling 10 cells from each of the 38 cell types, resulting in 380 carefully selected samples ( Supplementary Table 4 ). During the sampling process, we enforced a constraint that the gene-word overlap between any two cell sentences must not exceed 50%, in order to enhance sample diversity and improve reasoning generalizability.\n\n387\n\n388\n\n389\n\n390\n\n391\n\n392\n\n393\n\n394\n\n395\n\n396\n\n397\n\n398\n\n399\n\n400\n\n401\n\n402\n\n403\n\n404\n\n405\n\n406\n\n407\n\n408\n\n409\n\n410\n\n411\n\n412\n\n413\n\n414\n\n415\n\n416\n\n417\n\n418\n\n419\n\n420\n\nSecond, to support training in the knowledge infusion stage, we randomly selected 1,000 cells per cell type, resulting in a pancancer38k training dataset consisting of 37,187 cells ( Supplementary Table 5 ). For cell types with fewer than 1,000 available cells, we retained 100 cells for internal evaluation and used the remainder for training.\n\nFinally, we randomly selected an additional 3,800 cells to construct an internal test set ( Supplementary Table 6 ), ensuring that there was no overlap with the training data. This dataset was used for downstream model evaluation and analysis. [truncated]","original_chars":1630},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"Datasets","heading_level":2,"line_start":1145,"line_end":1162,"text":"### PDAC dataset\n\nThe PDAC dataset (Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma) comprises 57,702 single-cell transcriptomes encompassing 51 distinct cell subtypes. It includes a broad spectrum of immune and non-immune cell types, such as CD8+ T cells, NKT cells, CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, monocytes, neutrophils, fibroblasts, epithelial cells (EC), endothelial cells, and B cells. The dataset also contains various macrophage and T cell subsets, endocrine cells, acinar cells, T follicular helper (TFH) cells, and mast cells. Notably, macrophage subtypes include LAM1-4, M1-like, and M2-like macrophages.\n\n### PBMC3K dataset\n\nThe PBMC3K dataset contains single-cell RNA-seq data from 2,700 peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs) derived from a healthy human donor. The dataset includes major immune cell populations such as naïve CD4+ T cells, memory CD4+ T cells, CD14+ monocytes, B cells, CD8+ T cells, FCGR3A+ monocytes, natural killer (NK) cells, dendritic cells (DCs), and platelets.\n\n### Multiome PBMC dataset\n\nThis dataset contains paired single-cell RNA and ATAC-seq profiles from human PBMCs, generated using the 10x Genomics Single Cell Multiome platform. [truncated]","original_chars":2099},{"section_type":"data_representation","heading":"dataset","heading_level":3,"line_start":1147,"line_end":1150,"text":"The PDAC dataset (Pancreatic Ductal Adenocarcinoma) comprises 57,702 single-cell transcriptomes encompassing 51 distinct cell subtypes. It includes a broad spectrum of immune and non-immune cell types, such as CD8+ T cells, NKT cells, CD4+ T cells, plasma cells, monocytes, neutrophils, fibroblasts, epithelial cells (EC), endothelial cells, and B cells. The dataset also contains various macrophage and T cell subsets, endocrine cells, acinar cells, T follicular helper (TFH) cells, and mast cells. Notably, macrophage subtypes include LAM1-4, M1-like, and M2-like macrophages.","original_chars":578}],"docling_markdown":"data/docling_include_no_vlm_2026-07-09/markdown/full_2026_07_06_rec_002581.md"}]

